---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { pages } from '../data/pages';
import { exhibitRecord, philympiaList, philympiaSources } from '../data/exhibitions';
import { imageFor, pageHref } from '../lib/site';

// First exhibit page (frames 1–9) showing a given stamp.
const linkFor = (id: string, prefer?: string) => {
  const list = pages.filter((p) => p.frame <= 9 && p.stamps.includes(id));
  const hit = (prefer && list.find((p) => p.kinds.includes(prefer as never))) || list[0];
  return hit ? pageHref(hit) : undefined;
};
const sheets = [imageFor(11, 1), imageFor(11, 2)];
const frenchArt = exhibitRecord.filter((r) => r.frenchArt);
---
<Base title="Philympia 1970 and the exhibition record" description="Milton Kohn’s handwritten list of the Famous French Arts frames sent to Philympia 1970 in London, and the Kohns’ documented exhibition awards 1967–1984." image={sheets[0].md}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header style="padding: 48px 0 24px; max-width: 800px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">London, 18–26 September 1970</p>
      <h1>Philympia 1970</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Among the papers kept with the collection are two sheets in Milton Kohn’s hand headed
        <em>“Famous French Arts sent to ‘Philympia’, the International Stamp Exhibition at Olympia, London, Eng.”</em>
        They list, frame by frame, the pages he shipped to London. They are the earliest record of the exhibit travelling abroad.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section style="padding-top:0">
      <div class="split">
        {sheets.map((img, i) => (
          <figure class="mounted" style="margin:0">
            <a href={img.lg}><img src={img.md} alt={`Milton Kohn’s handwritten Philympia inventory, sheet ${i + 1}`} width={img.width} height={img.height} loading="lazy" /></a>
            <figcaption style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;color:var(--ink-soft);padding:10px 4px 2px">
              Sheet {i + 1}. Kohn’s pencilled valuations are blurred online; they are not blurred on the original.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="eyebrow">The show</p>
          <h2>A world exhibition at Olympia</h2>
          <p>
            Philympia 1970 was an international stamp exhibition under the patronage of the Fédération Internationale de
            Philatélie. It was held in the Empire Hall at Olympia, London, from 18 to 26 September 1970, and organised by the
            Royal Philatelic Society London, the British Philatelic Association and the Philatelic Traders’ Society.
          </p>
          <p>
            It was very large. More than 7,000 applications were received for about 3,600 frames of sixteen pages each, and all
            the frames were allotted nearly nine months before opening. A jury of 31 judged the exhibits; the American jurors
            were John H. Boker Jr., Lester G. Brookman and Edith Faulstich. American entries were handled by U.S. commissioners,
            the New York dealers Ezra D. Cole, Bernard D. Harmer and Robert A. Siegel.
          </p>
          <p>
            Thematic (“subject”) exhibits were judged in their own class. The <em>American Philatelist</em> reported that 127
            U.S. residents won awards, and that the best result for an American thematic exhibit was Large Silver. The Royal Mail
            issued three Philympia stamps on the opening day, designed by David Gentleman, and a different special handstamp
            was used on each day of the show.
          </p>
          <p>
            Kohn’s frames were exactly the Philympia size: sixteen pages each. So far no printed record has been found of the
            award, if any, that <em>Famous French Arts</em> received there. The full palmarès (a 23-page booklet) and the
            exhibition catalogue are held by philatelic libraries and are the next place to look.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">What he sent — and where it is now</p>
          <h2>Kohn’s list, linked to this site</h2>
          <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.9rem;color:var(--ink-soft)">Each line of the inventory links to the album page where that item appears today.</p>
          {([['Frame 1 — stamps, first day covers and sketches', philympiaList.frame1, 'First day cover'], ['Frame 2 — proofs, errors and special cancels', philympiaList.frame2, 'Signed artist’s proof'], ['Third sheet — additional pages', philympiaList.frame3, 'Deluxe proof']] as const).map(([h, items, pref]) => (
            <section class="record" style="padding:0">
              <h3>{h}</h3>
              <ol style="margin:0;padding:12px 16px 12px 36px;font-size:.95rem">
                {items.map(([label, id]) => {
                  const href = linkFor(id, pref);
                  return <li>{href ? <a href={href}>{label}</a> : label}</li>;
                })}
              </ol>
            </section>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="record">
      <p class="eyebrow">The exhibits on the road</p>
      <h2>The Kohns’ exhibition record, 1967–1984</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The awards below were found in the stamp press of the time, mainly <em>The American Philatelist</em>, the
          <em>Polonus Bulletin</em> and the <em>Wisconsin Federation of Stamp Clubs Bulletin</em>, through the American Philatelic
          Research Library’s digital archive. Each row links to the printed page. Rows marked ★ are for the French art exhibit shown on this
          site. The others are the Kohns’ postal-history exhibits of concentration-camp mail, the collecting for which Milton
          Kohn is best known.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:16px">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem;min-width:820px">
          <thead>
            <tr style="text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-soft)">
              <th style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Year</th>
              <th style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Exhibition</th>
              <th style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Exhibitor</th>
              <th style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Exhibit</th>
              <th style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Award</th>
              <th style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Source (printed page)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {exhibitRecord.map((r) => (
              <tr style={r.frenchArt ? 'background:#f6ecee' : ''}>
                <td style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);white-space:nowrap">{r.frenchArt ? '★ ' : ''}{r.year}</td>
                <td style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">{r.show}</td>
                <td style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">{r.who}</td>
                <td style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">{r.exhibit}</td>
                <td style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">{r.award}</td>
                <td style="padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem"><a href={r.source.url} rel="noopener">{r.source.label.replace('APRL digital library — ', '')}</a></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="split" style="margin-top:40px">
        <blockquote class="hand" style="margin:0">
          {frenchArt.filter((r) => r.quote).map((r) => (
            <p>“{r.quote}.” <span style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.75rem;color:var(--ink-soft)">— {r.source.label.replace('APRL digital library — ', '')}</span></p>
          ))}
          <cite>The French art exhibit in the stamp press</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>How it all connects</h3>
          <p>
            <em>Famous French Arts</em> was on the exhibition circuit within two years of Milton Kohn taking up stamps: in 1967
            at Park Forest, Illinois, judges gave it a gold medal. After Philympia it won the Milwaukee Philatelic Society
            President’s Award in 1975 and a silver at POLPEX ’76 in Chicago. In 1981 “French Art on Stamps” won a bronze at the
            international ROCPEX in Taipei, where Milton was one of the U.S. commissioners. The signed dinner card from Taipei in
            the <a href="/frames/9/15/">miscellany</a> dates from that trip.
          </p>
          <p>
            Janet Kohn was an exhibitor in her own right. In November 1978 she won the Grand Award, best in show, at SUPEX ’78, the show of
            the Suburban Collectors Club of Chicago at Brookfield, Illinois, for “Concentration Camp Censor Markings”. She won
            international medals at Amsterdam, Prague, Vienna and Taipei.
          </p>
          <p>
            The judges noticed the hand-drawn artwork on the Kohns’ pages from the start. A 1967 report on Milton’s airmail
            exhibit said it “drew the acclaim of the judges”. That artwork is on almost every page of this collection.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section style="padding-top:0">
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem">Sources</h2>
      <ol class="sources">
        {philympiaSources.map((s) => <li><a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.label}</a></li>)}
        {exhibitRecord.filter((r) => r.source.url.startsWith('http')).map((r) => <li><a href={r.source.url} rel="noopener">{r.source.label}</a> ({r.show}, {r.year})</li>)}
      </ol>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
